<p>
Better late than never. Somehow it just seems that life gets busier and busier, doesn't it?
</p>

<p>
<a href="https://sachachua.com/blog/2025/08/turning-42-life-as-a-41-year-old/">Last year</a>, I wrote:
</p>

<blockquote>
<p>
I wonder what things will be like in a year, in five years, in ten years. I think things are going to be harder, not easier. I want to use this time to store up good memories and prepare for the storm. I want to keep developing relationships, playfulness, and practical life skills. I want to stay flexible and practise acceptance. There will likely be some tough decisions in my near future. It sucks, but other people have made it through before, and we can too.
</p>
</blockquote>

<p>
The storm did come in the form of health challenges for people that I
care about, and we did make it through. There were moments when I felt
the weight of stress on me, but I took it slowly, dropped things that
were not essential, and asked for help. It's not over, of course.
There's more to come. But I think we're starting from a pretty solid
foundation, and that's going to help.
</p>

<p>
Back then, I had been thinking about:
</p>

<ul class="org-ul">
<li>How can I get better at supporting A+ when she wants help and step back when she wants to try things on her own?
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>I've been taking a step back and letting her manage classwork, homework, attendance, participation, all of those things. This has been working out better for us than otherwise. Sure, there are days when I feel a bit nervous about letting her sleep in so many times in a week. But ultimately, she's going to have to be the one in charge, and it's better to figure out if she needs or wants help while the stakes are low.</li>
</ul></li>
<li>How can I get better at managing myself so that I can enjoy the time she wants to spend with me and pounce on the opportunities to do my own things when she wants to be independent?
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>When things go smoothly, I now have a couple of hours of focus time during schooldays that I can use to code and write and draw. This makes it easier for me to enjoy spending time with the kiddo when she's out of school, because I know that I can postpone any ideas to the next day.</li>
</ul></li>
<li>What do I really value, and what am I willing to sacrifice?
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>I'd rather help the kiddo figure out something that works well for her long term, even if it might mean dealing with other people's opinions on parenting decisions.</li>
<li>I'd rather prioritize our long-term health, even if it means figuring out different ways to be present when people are going through a hard time.</li>
</ul></li>
<li>What's in my control? What can I influence? What's outside both?
<ul class="org-ul">
<li>The outside world has gotten even messier, but that's okay, we'll just keep plugging along. I can learn more about connecting with local communities and seeing how to help there too.</li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
