<p></p>

<p>
This year, it's been satisfying seeing people grow into themselves.
The kiddo is ten, as she often likes to remind me.
She's figured a lot of things out.
She's been enjoying gymnastics and Pokémon.
Attending virtual school is still not one of her favorite things to do, but she's proud of her ability to do well.
We've been talking a lot more about neurodivergence and figuring out the kinds of things that work for you.
We regularly use earbuds to manage sensory overstimulation in noisy environments and lots of hugs to feel warm and cozy.
I love seeing her overhearing her negotiate with her teacher for an introvert breakout room when the students are playing Gimkit because her classmates tend to yell in their excitement and she finds it a bit loud.
</p>

<p>
I started learning French because the kiddo started taking it in school, and that's been working out really well too.
I love the exercise of getting my brain to do things, and I've been having a lot of fun tinkering with my tools and processes as well.
My daughter and I have gotten to the point of being able to enjoy going through some of the simpler graphic novels for series that we've read so many times in English.
I've also been able to go to things like Emacs Paris online, and I had four Emacs Chats in French.
</p>

<p>
This year, I had a lot of fun hanging out with the kiddo.
I think we spent more time together playing together than in previous years because we've found more things that we both enjoy.
We had started playing Dungeons and Dragons with her cousins, but even when they got busy, we continued with our own adventures.
Now we have all these adventures we've built up around her pet owlbear.
I DM for her and I also play a fighter so that someone's around to tank melee attacks.
AI has been pretty handy for quickly resolving questions about rules and generating story ideas.
In addition to having lots of dice at home, I can can also roll virtual dice on my phone.
This lets us tell stories wherever we are, including when we're waiting in line for roller coasters. This summer, we went to Canada's Wonderland a lot. Turns out the kiddo loves coasters and other thrill rides, so that's been a great way to spend time outside.
</p>
